  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Friday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Wraparound showers from an upper level\nshortwave exiting the Great Basin to the northeast are \ndiminishing across northeastern Elko County this afternoon as \nevidenced by satellite and radar imagery. Under northwest post\nfrontal flow fair weather conditions are expected for the \nremainder of the weekend in absence of any significant systems \nover the area. \n\nAnother low pressure system off the CA coast will begin to trek\neastward late Sunday night into Monday. The LPC will cross CA and\nenter southern NV by Monday advecting vorticity and sufficient\nmoisture for shower and thunderstorm activity across central and \neastern NV Monday evening and Tuesday. Better agreement for \nmoisture availability exists with the most recent model run \namongst long term numerical models. PW values of 0.5-0.7 inches \nwill push northward on the strength of southerly flow in the \nsystems downstream across Eureka, White Pine and Elko Counties on \nTuesday. Current guidance has more storm activity across central \nNV on Tuesday than further north. ECMWF guidance has rainfall \namounts of 0.1-0.2 inches across eastern NV while GFS has amounts \na little more robust between 0.3 and 0.5 inches. \n\nThe system will exit the area on Wednesday riding the flow of its\nparent trough centered off the western Canadian coast. Brief\nridging with an HPC to the south over western AZ will set up over\nthe SW CONUS through the rest of the work week leading to a\ngradual warming trend through Friday. Afternoon high temperatures\nwill climb back into the low to mid 80s. Meanwhile the next\nshortwave riding the parent ULT to the north will strengthen over\nthe Pacific Ocean and begin to make its way onshore by the weekend\nfollowing a similar trajectory as the early week system. \n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High confidence remains in a\nquiet forecast through Monday morning. Low confidence exists in\nshower and thunderstorm activity on Tuesday as well as the warming\ntrend for later next week. No major grid edits needed at this\ntime.",
